---
/**
 * The materials page.
 *
 * The design system's version listed European oak, American black walnut and
 * painted tulipwood, with quarter-sawn grain references and a swatch each. The
 * confirmed range (Q22, 30 Aug 2026) is engineered board throughout: five
 * families, each carrying its Q39 description from `site-content.ts`. Q22's
 * ruling also stands: who cuts the material, and the arrangement behind it,
 * stays off the site.
 *
 * No swatches yet. The design mapped its chips onto --oak, --walnut and
 * --green, and reusing those to stand for materials that are not those timbers
 * would put the removed claim back through the side door: a chip filled with
 * oak brown and captioned MDF still says oak. Q24 is now answered, so choosing
 * real swatches is unblocked, but it is a design decision not yet made.
 *
 * Q43 is the argument this page has to win, and it is his: engineered board
 * over solid timber as a considered choice, in his reasoning rather than ours.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Wrap from "../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import Display from "../components/ds/Display.astro";
import Kicker from "../components/ds/Kicker.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import MaterialChip from "../components/ds/MaterialChip.astro";
import EnquireBand from "../components/ds/EnquireBand.astro";
import MonoLabel from "../components/ds/MonoLabel.astro";
import Value from "../components/Value.astro";

import { MATERIALS, BAND, CONTACT } from "../data/site-content";
import { indexable } from "../data/page-gates";

const isIndexable = indexable("/materials/");

---

<Base
  title="Materials and finishes"
  description="What bespoke fitted furniture is made from, what each material is like to work with, and why engineered board is the right choice for fitted work."
  noindex={!isIndexable}
>
  <Wrap style="padding-top:var(--hero-y);padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <Kicker scribe>Materials</Kicker>
    <Display level={1} as="h1" style="margin:var(--space-12) 0 var(--space-13)">
      What it is made from
    </Display>
    {/* Both paragraphs are Kaspar's, 6 September 2026: the first under a
        screenshot of this heading (13:40), the second under "Materials &
        finishes" three minutes later. Ours, about a material having to hold a
        line against a wall that moves, went with the first.

        GROOVED AND SLATTED FEATURE PANELS were new to the site on this date.
        They are now the Decorative and feature panels card below, in his own
        words, so the paragraph and the card agree. */}
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);font-size:var(--type-lede);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      Material choice affects how fitted furniture looks, feels and performs over time. We specify
      each material for its stability, durability and finish, choosing what is best suited to the
      design and how the furniture will be used.
    </p>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-10);font-size:var(--type-lede);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      Our material range includes moisture-resistant MDF, natural wood veneer, melamine and
      decorative boards, as well as grooved, slatted and other feature panels. These can be
      combined within a project to achieve the right balance of appearance, durability and cost.
    </p>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Every material, hardware included, 2 Sep 2026 on Denis's ask. Hardware
      used to be held out of this grid so it would not appear twice on one
      page. That left five cards in a three-column grid and an empty sixth slot
      staring back, which is a worse problem than the one it solved. It sits
      here now, last, filling that slot, and the section below keeps its copy
      without repeating the card.

      Each card opens with where the material is used and what weighs for and
      against it (added 1 Sep 2026). */}
  <Wrap style="padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <SectionHead
      meta={`${MATERIALS.filter((m) => m.key !== "hardware").length} boards and the hardware`}
      scribe
    >
      The range
    </SectionHead>

    {/* Three over two, the last two cards spanning the row at the same
        height as the three above: see the note on the same grid in
        index.astro. */}
    <div class="mat-grid">
      {MATERIALS.map((m, i) => (
        <MaterialChip
          name={m.name}
          swatch={m.swatch}
          {/* The hardware swatch is a photograph of parts, not a finish, so it
              keeps no pure band above the wash. See MaterialChip. */}
          band={m.key !== "hardware"}
          description={m.description}
          uses={m.uses}
          pros={m.pros}
          cons={m.cons}
          class={i >= MATERIALS.length - 2 ? "wide" : undefined}
          sizes={i >= MATERIALS.length - 2 ? "(max-width: 700px) 92vw, (max-width: 1080px) 92vw, 480px" : undefined}
        />
      ))}
    </div>

    {/* "The full board range", Q22 rendered as a list, came off here on
        6 September 2026. His range paragraph in the hero now says what the
        range is, and the Q22 list named plywood, which he took out of the
        range the same afternoon. Q22 stays in this page's gate. */}
  </Wrap>

  {/* THE THREE SECTIONS BELOW ARE HIS, 6 September 2026, each typed under a
      screenshot of the section it replaces (13:45, 13:47, 13:50). They stood
      on Q24, Q28, Q43 and Q40 until then; those answers are unchanged in
      answers.ts and still gate the page, and his later sentences on the same
      facts are what a visitor reads. Em dashes written as commas. */}
  <Wrap style="padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <SectionHead meta="Finishes" level={4}>How it is finished</SectionHead>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
      Painted furniture can be hand-painted or spray-finished in a wide range of colours. Natural
      wood veneer can be left in its natural tone or stained to achieve the colour and character
      desired, from light and subtle to rich, dark finishes. Factory-finished decorative boards
      offer a further range of colours, wood effects and textures.
    </p>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-10);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      The right finish depends on the design, how the furniture will be used and the overall look
      you want to achieve.
    </p>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Named brands allowed here by Denis's ruling, 30 Aug 2026: Blum and Häfele
      are what he actually specifies (Q28), and buyers recognise them. The
      cutting arrangement stays unnamed, which is the client's own Q22 ruling.
      His heading for this section was "The parts you use every day". */}
  <Wrap style="padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <SectionHead meta="Hardware" level={4}>The parts you use every day</SectionHead>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
      We specify quality hardware from established manufacturers such as Blum and Häfele,
      alongside other specialist systems where the project requires them. Soft-close hinges and
      drawer systems are included as standard where appropriate.
    </p>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-10)">
      Good hardware affects how furniture feels in everyday use: doors close smoothly, drawers run
      properly and components can be precisely adjusted to stay aligned. It costs more than basic
      alternatives, but provides better operation and greater durability over the life of the
      furniture.
    </p>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-10);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      Hinges, drawer runners and other moving hardware are also covered by their respective
      manufacturer warranties, alongside our own workmanship guarantee.
    </p>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style="padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <SectionHead meta="The one worth asking about" level={4}>Why not solid timber</SectionHead>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
      Solid timber has its place, but for most fitted furniture engineered boards offer greater
      stability and consistency. MR MDF is ideal for painted furniture, while natural wood veneer
      gives the character of real timber with far less solid hardwood.
    </p>
    {/* New to the site, and in no questionnaire answer: the source is him,
        in writing, 6 September 2026. */}
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-10);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      We consider sustainability in the materials we specify, aiming to use them efficiently,
      minimise waste and choose responsibly sourced products wherever possible.
    </p>
  </Wrap>

  <EnquireBand heading="If you have a room in mind, start here." chamfer>
    {BAND.body}
    <Fragment slot="facts">
      {BAND.facts.map((q) => (
        <MonoLabel size="md" tone="paper-soft" style="letter-spacing:.1em">
          <Value q={q} />
        </MonoLabel>
      ))}
    </Fragment>
  </EnquireBand>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Three over two, the same rules as the homepage grid with this page's
     wider gap: see index.astro for the reasoning. */
  .mat-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--gap-lg);
    align-items: start;
    container-type: inline-size;
  }
  .mat-grid > :global(*) {
    grid-column: span 2;
  }
  .mat-grid > :global(.wide) {
    grid-column: span 3;
    --mat-ratio: 9 / 4;
  }
  /* Frame height derived from the grid width, so the stretched tiles are
     wider and never taller: the reasoning is beside the same rule in
     index.astro. This page's gap is --gap-lg. */
  .mat-grid :global(.ss-mat-frame) {
    height: calc((100cqw - 2 * var(--gap-lg)) / 4.5);
  }
  @media (max-width: 1080px) {
    .mat-grid > :global(*),
    .mat-grid > :global(.wide) {
      grid-column: span 3;
      --mat-ratio: 3 / 2;
    }
    .mat-grid > :global(.wide):last-child {
      grid-column: span 6;
      --mat-ratio: 9 / 4;
    }
    .mat-grid :global(.ss-mat-frame) {
      height: calc((100cqw - var(--gap-lg)) / 3);
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 660px) {
    .mat-grid > :global(*),
    .mat-grid > :global(.wide),
    .mat-grid > :global(.wide):last-child {
      grid-column: span 6;
      --mat-ratio: 3 / 2;
    }
    .mat-grid :global(.ss-mat-frame) {
      height: calc(100cqw / 1.5);
    }
  }
</style>
